==In fiction and literature==
Naval Base San Diego is the setting for the 2005 naval thriller novel ''[[Treason (legal thriller)|Treason]]'' by [[Don Brown (author, born 1960)|Don Brown]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Brown |first1=Don |author1-link=Don Brown (author, born 1960) |title=Treason |date=2009 |publisher=Zondervan |isbn=978-0310542681 |page=16 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bOpX-z0cnFAC&dq=treason%20by%20don%20brown%2032nd%20street&pg=PA16 |access-date=12 July 2021|quote=reference to ''32nd Street Naval Station'' in novel}}</ref>

==Aerial view==
The Navy has promised to alter the design of one of the buildings on site, which from above clearly resembles a [[Nazi swastika]] pitched at the correct 45-degree angle the Nazis used. A report by [[SFGATE]] suggests that top officials in the Navy knew what the design of the building would look like prior to the installation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=SFGATE |first=Andrew Chamings |date=2022-06-19 |title=The notorious history of California's swastika-shaped building |url=https://www.sfgate.com/obscuresf/article/history-of-California-swastika-building-17241331.php |access-date=2023-05-09 |website=SFGATE |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2007-09-26 |title=Navy to fix swastika-shaped barracks in Calif |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-navy-swastika-idUSN2623711220070926 |access-date=2023-05-09}}</ref>

